          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            The theory of solid modules was used to formulate the correct theory of
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Langlands+correspondence"} local Langlands correspondence
            by Fargues and Scholze (
            %a{:href => "#FarguesScholze21"}> FarguesScholze21
            ). It has also been used by Mann to formalize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
            (
            %a{:href => "#Mann22"}> Mann22
            ).
          %p
            Condensed mathematics has also been used in
            %a{:href => "#ClausenScholze22"} ClausenScholze22
            to provide new “analysis-free” proofs of the finiteness of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+cohomology"} coherent cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+duality"} Serre duality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GAGA"} GAGA
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hirzebruch-Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Hirzebruch-Riemann-Roch theorem
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analysis"} complex analysis
